+# Decisions taken for you — the design phase
+
+One line per panel decision (decision 21): the item, the reviewer's and the devil's advocate's verdicts, the conciliator's decision and rationale, the cost. A review comment on any of them overturns it (/sdlc-fix); an overturned line says so.
+
+- 1. [concern] Spot REST host and refused fills: the design picks `data-api.binance.vision` as the spot — reviewer: Accept the design as written (close the concern): `data-api.binance.vision` as the fixed spot default and 451/other-4xx refusals reported as gaps with the status.; devil's advocate: Keep `data-api.binance.vision` as the spot default, but do not close the concern as-is: the build must add an allow-listed host override and must not let the 418 guard depend on a header the mirror may not send.; decision: Keep data-api.binance.vision as the fixed spot REST host (no switch) and refused fills (451, any non-418 4xx) as gaps with the status; the weight pause must treat a missing X-MBX-USED-WEIGHT-1M as unknown and pause conservatively, pinned by an offline no-header test.; rationale: Both verdicts agree on the mirror as default and on refusals-as-gaps; the intent names both hosts and proposes 'gaps with the reason', and 418 still stops the run. The host override is not taken: the intent asks for no option, coding-standards rule 3 forbids unasked scope, and a later small change can add it if the owner wants it. They disagree on the 418 guard; the safer reading wins: the intent's hard rule is never get the IP banned, and a pause keyed to a header the mirror may not send would be dead code. A conservative no-header path plus a pinned test closes that gap without widening scope or touching any guardrail.; cost: n/a
+- 2. [concern] Storage layout and codec level not measured: the intent asked the design to measure one — reviewer: Accept the deferral and close the concern as decided: keep one zstd-3 file per day; the build records the measurement as planned (plan step 10) and the owner judges the figure. Not an Important finding.; devil's advocate: Do not accept the deferral as written: close the concern at design with a reasoned decision on layout and codec level, and turn the build-time "measurement" into either reproducible evidence or an owner-run check after merge.; decision: Keep one zstd level-3 Parquet file per day as the intent requires (line 59); the build commits the generator that produces evidence/storage-measurement.md (per-day vs one yearly file, plus a higher zstd level) so the figure is reproducible and labelled synthetic, and the spec gives the owner the real-data check (download BTCUSDT 1m for one year, then cache-info) with a reopen threshold of more than 20% over a single yearly file.; rationale: Both verdicts agree to keep per-day files; intent line 59 fixes one file per day written atomically, so a month or year layout would contradict a stated requirement. Neither design nor build can fetch real BTCUSDT data (no allowed domains), so the intent's measurement (line 161) can only be synthetic in-run or real on the owner's machine. They disagree on whether an uncommitted synthetic figure counts as evidence; the safer reading is the advocate's: commit the generator so a verifier can re-run it, and state that the data is synthetic. The owner's real-data check with an explicit threshold gives the per-day choice a defined way to be reopened; zstd 3 stays as the default because the codec choice changes no schema or behaviour. No limit, guardrail or risk-list item is touched; this only shapes the evidence for this item.; cost: n/a
+- 3. [concern] Assumptions where the intent is silent (A1–A12 in Design § Assumptions), the weightiest — reviewer: Accept A1–A12 as written and have the owner close the concern as decided; no assumption breaks behaviour, leaks data or breaches a policy, and A10 is what the intent requires.; devil's advocate: Do not close A1–A12 as "design choices the owner accepts at merge": A10 makes the exception the normal path for real Binance data and the Proof list never tests it; require an explicit, non-silent opt-in for intra-file holes and an acceptance test for A10 before the concern is closed.; decision: A1–A12 accepted as written, A10 keeps raising KlineGapError by default; Gap.reason tells a candle missing inside a source file apart from a day absent from both sources, and plan.md Proof gains a test with a fixture missing two consecutive candles that asserts the exact Gap start/end/candles/reason and the .frame rows in schema; no allow_gaps keyword is added; rationale: Both agree A10's raise is the intent's own rule (intent Sources: "never a silently short frame"); A1–A9, A11 and A12 are uncontested. The advocate's proof hole is a checkable fact: plan.md Proof lists only test_day_missing_from_both_sources_raises_gap_error, nothing for an in-file hole. The safer reading closes the concern only with that behaviour proven, so the test and the distinct reason are required now. The allow_gaps keyword is left out: KlineGapError.frame already gives a non-silent route to the partial data, and a new API surface is a scope change for the owner to request.; cost: n/a
+
+Total panel cost: n/a
